      <h2 style="margin-top:0">Why a paid audit, when the free one exists</h2>
      <p>The <a href="/audit/" class="inline-link">free 5-point audit</a> is exactly what it says: five points, written report, no sales call required. It is a real deliverable. It also has a ceiling &mdash; it covers the homepage and one service page, not the whole site, and it does not include a recorded walk-through.</p>
      <p>The Service Site Audit is the same posture, deeper:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Five top pages</strong> instead of two. Homepage, top three service pages, top service-area page. Real Lighthouse runs on each, mobile + desktop.</li>
        <li><strong>Full schema audit.</strong> Every JSON-LD block on the site checked for syntax, completeness, GBP entity match, and missed opportunities.</li>
        <li><strong>Mobile-form audit.</strong> Every form on the site evaluated: field count, intake intelligence, urgency routing, accessibility, conversion friction.</li>
        <li><strong>Local-pack diagnostic.</strong> NAP consistency between site and Google Business Profile. Citation cross-check across major directories. Service-area page depth scored.</li>
        <li><strong>Conversion-flow review.</strong> Every CTA path traced. Every emergency / phone-click / form-fill flow tested on a real phone.</li>
        <li><strong>60-minute recorded walk-through.</strong> Not a written summary &mdash; a Loom video walking through every finding, on screen, with my voice explaining what to fix and what to leave alone.</li>
        <li><strong>"Fix these three" prioritization.</strong> The audit ends with a one-page priority list: the three things to fix this month, the three things to fix next quarter, and the three things to ignore.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Who this is for</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Service-business owners with an existing site spending real money on Google Ads or organic SEO and wondering why the leads are flat.</li>
        <li>Agencies and consultants who want a senior-developer second opinion on a client's site before recommending a rebuild.</li>
        <li>Buyers who would book a discovery call but want a deeper diagnostic in writing first.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What you get, day-by-day</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Day 0 (you):</strong> submit the form below. Web3Forms emails me your URL plus business context. Stripe sends the invoice; payment is required to start.</li>
        <li><strong>Day 1 (me):</strong> Lighthouse runs on five pages, schema audit, NAP cross-check. Findings logged.</li>
        <li><strong>Day 3 (me):</strong> conversion-flow review on a real phone. Form intelligence audit. Local-pack diagnostic.</li>
        <li><strong>Day 5 (me):</strong> 25-page report drafted in Google Docs.</li>
        <li><strong>Day 7 (me):</strong> 60-minute walk-through recorded in Loom.</li>
        <li><strong>Day 10 (you):</strong> PDF report + Loom link delivered by email. 30-minute Q&amp;A call available on request, no extra cost.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Refund posture</h2>
      <p>If within 60 days of the audit you sign on for the monthly plan or the lump-sum, the $499 is credited back &mdash; either toward your first invoice (monthly) or off the lump-sum total. You only pay for the audit if you decide it stands on its own.</p>
      <p>If the audit is delivered and you do not feel it was worth $499, email me and I refund it. No questions, no friction. The audit lives or dies on whether it teaches you something concrete.</p>
